---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import PageHero from "../../components/PageHero.astro";
import CompareTable from "../../components/CompareTable.astro";
import SiteCheckForm from "../../components/SiteCheckForm.astro";

const rows = [
  { label: "Declares provenance for files that carry the credential", them: "Yes", us: "-" },
  { label: "Survives text being copied out of a page", them: "No", us: "Yes" },
  { label: "Protects your text once it leaves your store", them: "No", us: "Yes" },
  { label: "Proves a stolen description was yours", them: "No", us: "Yes" },
  { label: "Produces evidence for a DMCA takedown", them: "No", us: "Yes" },
];
---

<Base
  title="Content Credentials vs Kirpik"
  description="A plain comparison of C2PA Content Credentials with Kirpik: what provenance metadata does, whether it prevents content theft, and why text copied out of a page carries no metadata at all."
>
  <PageHero
    title="Content Credentials vs Kirpik"
    lead="The Coalition for Content Provenance and Authenticity (C2PA) defines Content Credentials: signed metadata that records where a file came from and how it was edited. The credential travels beside the content, not inside it, and that decides what it can do about theft."
  />

  <section class="section" style="padding-top:0">
    <div class="container narrow prose">
      <h2>What Content Credentials are</h2>
      <p>
        A credential is a signed record attached to a file. It states who made the file and what edits
        it has been through, and software that understands the format can verify the signatures. The
        standard grew up in photography and news publishing as a way to show that an image is what it
        claims to be, and that is the job it is designed for.
      </p>
      <p>
        One app in Shopify's anti-theft category takes this route. Mintall Keep's listing, as recorded
        in July 2026, offers "C2PA Content Credentials" and provenance records with "no badges or
        watermarks". It is compared in full <a href="/compare/mintallkeep/">on its own page</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Does it prevent content theft?</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>No.</strong> A credential declares provenance; it does not stop anyone taking the
        content, and it does not stop Shopify serving your text through the machine-readable feeds
        (JSON, XML and ATOM) that scrapers actually read. The credential travels beside the content
        rather than inside it: it is stripped when text is copied out of a page, a scraper reading a
        product feed takes the description field and nothing attached beside it, and platforms
        routinely discard file metadata when they re-encode uploaded images.
      </p>
      <p>
        None of this is a defect in C2PA. The standard's own remit is provenance disclosure for content
        that still carries its credentials; a thief's version is the content that does not.
      </p>
      <p>
        Kirpik does not claim to prevent content theft: it <strong>protects</strong> content. The
        watermark travels inside the text itself, so stolen content stays identifiable and provable
        wherever it surfaces. Kirpik adds no scripts to your storefront, so it runs alongside any
        blocker without conflict.
      </p>

      <h2>Can it prove infringement?</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>No, not for stolen text.</strong> A credential can show where content that still
        carries it came from; the stolen version is the version that does not, because provenance
        metadata is stripped when text is copied out of a page.
      </p>
      <p>
        Kirpik proves infringement through the watermark and the Proof of Ownership Certificate.
        Detection reads your watermark out of the stolen text, and the certificate records dated
        fingerprints, the infringing page's timestamps, Wayback Machine corroboration and a public
        verification page, ready for a DMCA takedown.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" style="padding-top:0">
    <div class="container narrow">
      <h2 class="tablehead">Content Credentials vs Kirpik</h2>
      <CompareTable themLabel="Content Credentials" rows={rows} />
    </div>
  </section>

  <SiteCheckForm tone="band" heading="Is your store's content protected?" cta="Check my store">
    <Fragment slot="sub">
      Do you use <strong>Content Credentials</strong> or any other "Content Protection" App? Enter
      your Shopify store address to see if your content is actually protected.
    </Fragment>
  </SiteCheckForm>

  <section class="section" style="padding-top:0">
    <div class="container narrow">
      <section class="fineprint">
        <p>
          This page describes C2PA-style provenance metadata per the published standard's design.
          Metadata stripping is general, documented behaviour of clipboards and image re-encoding
          pipelines, not a defect of any app. Mintall Keep is quoted from its own public Shopify App
          Store listing as recorded in July 2026; listings change. App names and trademarks belong to
          their respective owners and are used for identification only. Nothing here is legal advice.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .tablehead { font-size: clamp(1.45rem, 1.15rem + 1.2vw, 2rem); }
  .fineprint { padding-top: 1.4rem; border-top: 1px solid var(--line); }
  .fineprint p { font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--ink-faint); line-height: 1.65; max-width: 78ch; }
  .fineprint a { color: var(--orange-deep); font-weight: 600; }
</style>
